diff --git a/packages/app-mobile/lists/nearby/PROTOCOL.md b/packages/app-mobile/lists/nearby/PROTOCOL.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b20f1d1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/app-mobile/lists/nearby/PROTOCOL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,251 @@
+# Nearby sync protocol (Bluetooth LE)
+
+This document describes the wire protocol used by transport 2 ("Nearby") from `docs/KONZEPT.md`
+section 3, for shopping-list sync between devices that are physically close, with no relay/internet
+required. It is written to be implementable on any platform; the Android implementation lives in
+`android/app/src/main/java/net/cozic/joplin/nearby/NearbyModule.kt` (native BLE primitives) and
+`lists/nearby/*.ts` (protocol, handshake, sync logic). This is the reference for a future iOS
+implementation (CoreBluetooth) -- iOS is not implemented in this round.
+
+## Service and characteristics
+
+- **Service UUID**: `d7a1e2b0-5f3c-4a9e-8b1d-6c2f9e4a7b3d` (constant, see `protocol.ts`).
+- **`rx` characteristic**: `d7a1e2b1-5f3c-4a9e-8b1d-6c2f9e4a7b3d`. Properties: WRITE, WRITE_NO_RESPONSE.
+  Direction: central -> peripheral. The peripheral's GATT server exposes it; the central writes
+  frames to it.
+- **`tx` characteristic**: `d7a1e2b2-5f3c-4a9e-8b1d-6c2f9e4a7b3d`. Properties: NOTIFY. Direction:
+  peripheral -> central. Has a standard Client Characteristic Configuration Descriptor (CCCD,
+  `00002902-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb`) that the central must write to subscribe.
+- Every device that wants to sync acts as **both** a BLE peripheral (GATT server, connectable
+  advertiser) and a BLE central (scanner, GATT client) at the same time -- see "Roles" below.
+
+## Advertising
+
+Each device advertises the service UUID plus 8 bytes of **service data**: an "advertising token"
+that identifies the list without revealing anything about it to a passive observer.
+
+- **Primary (legacy) advertising**: always on, 1M PHY, connectable. Every BLE 4.0+ central can see
+  and connect to it.
+- **Extended advertising set**: additionally started when the device and the OS support it
+  (`AdvertisingSetParameters` with primary PHY 1M / secondary PHY Coded), for longer range. Purely
+  additive -- the legacy advertisement keeps running regardless.
+- The advertising payload's service data is **not** the list id or any other stable identifier --
+  see "Token derivation" below. Advertising is deliberately not connectable-only-once; the same
+  advertisement is used to let multiple centrals discover and connect concurrently (a device may
+  end up connected to several peers for the same list).
+
+### Token derivation
+
+```
+window   = floor(now_ms / 900000)                     // 15-minute window index
+token    = HMAC-SHA256(contentKey, "adv" || window)[0..8]  // first 8 bytes
+```
+
+`contentKey` is the list's 32-byte symmetric content key (see `lists/core/crypto.ts` and
+`docs/KONZEPT.md` section 2/3) -- the same key used to encrypt item and directory events for
+relay sync. A device re-derives and re-advertises this token every 15 minutes
+(`updateToken()`/`TOKEN_ROTATION_MS`). A scanner checks a received token against the **current and
+previous** window of every locally open list's content key (clock-skew tolerance): if it matches,
+the advertiser is (very likely) a device open on the same list.
+
+This is a broadcast pre-shared-key scheme, not a strong authentication mechanism -- final trust
+is established by the handshake's proof exchange (below) plus the membership check, which the
+token can't forge.
+
+## Framing
+
+Every message (Hello, Proof, Summary, Event, Directory, Heartbeat, Done, Bye) is JSON, UTF-8
+encoded, then split into one or more **frames** that fit in a single GATT write/notification:
+
+```
+byte 0        message type   (1 byte)
+bytes 1-2     message id     (uint16, big-endian) -- identifies this (possibly multi-frame) message
+bytes 3-4     fragment index (uint16, big-endian) -- 0-based
+bytes 5-6     fragment count (uint16, big-endian) -- total fragments for this message
+bytes 7..     payload        (raw bytes of this fragment)
+```
+
+Header length: 7 bytes. Maximum payload per frame: `MTU - 3 (ATT overhead) - 7 (frame header)`.
+A message that fits in one frame still carries `count = 1, index = 0`.
+
+Message types (byte 0):
+
+| Value | Name      | Payload (JSON)                                               |
+|-------|-----------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
+| 1     | Hello     | `{ v, listId, pubkey, nonce, deviceName? }`                    |
+| 2     | Proof     | `{ proof }`                                                    |
+| 3     | Summary   | `{ items: [{ dTag, maxFieldTime }], directoryTime }`           |
+| 4     | Event     | `{ event }` -- a signed Nostr item event, see below            |
+| 5     | Directory | `{ event }` -- a signed Nostr directory event                  |
+| 6     | Heartbeat | `{}`                                                            |
+| 7     | Done      | `{}` -- "I've sent everything from my initial summary diff"    |
+| 8     | Bye       | `{}` -- graceful goodbye before disconnecting                  |
+
+### Reassembly
+
+A receiver keeps one partial-message buffer per `(connection, message id)`. A message is complete
+once all `count` fragments for its id have arrived (order-independent -- fragments may arrive out
+of order, though in practice a single sender's own frames are sent in order, one write at a time).
+A partial message that hasn't completed within **10 seconds** of its first fragment is dropped;
+later fragments for that same id then start a fresh message.
+
+## Roles: central and peripheral, both ways
+
+There's no fixed "server"/"client" designation for a list; every device both advertises
+(peripheral role) and scans (central role) at the same time. This means two devices in range of
+each other typically **both** discover **both** ways, and **both** initiate a `connect()`. On
+Android this can produce two independent links: one where device A is the GATT central (it called
+`connectGatt` on B) and simultaneously one where device A is the GATT peripheral (B connected to
+A's GATT server). The native module surfaces both under B's/A's Bluetooth address, without
+distinguishing which GATT role a given inbound event came from -- see `NearbyModule.kt`'s
+`emitConnected`/`emitFrame`, both keyed only by remote address.
+
+The nearby manager only starts **one** protocol session per remote address: a second `connected`
+event for an address that already has a session is treated as informational only (the link is
+already established from this module's point of view), not as an independent channel.
+
+### Duplicate-connection resolution
+
+If, despite the above, two independent sessions do end up open to the same peer (detected once
+both sides' `Hello` messages have been exchanged and reveal the same peer pubkey on two different
+addresses/connections), the rule is: **the connection whose central side has the numerically
+smaller pubkey (as a hex string) wins**; the other is disconnected. Both sides apply this rule
+independently and reach the same conclusion, since both know their own role (central/peripheral)
+on each connection and both pubkeys.
+
+## Handshake
+
+Both sides run the identical state machine (`session.ts`); there is no "initiator"/"responder"
+distinction beyond who happens to send `Hello` first (both send it unconditionally as soon as the
+connection is up).
+
+1. **A -> B**: `Hello { v: 1, listId, pubkey: pubkeyA, nonce: nonceA, deviceName? }`
+2. **B -> A**: `Hello { v: 1, listId, pubkey: pubkeyB, nonce: nonceB, deviceName? }`
+   (sent as soon as B's own connection is up, independent of whether A's Hello has arrived yet)
+3. Once each side has both nonces, both compute and send a **Proof**:
+   ```
+   proof = HMAC-SHA256(contentKey, "proof" || ownPubkey || nonceOfPeer || nonceOfOwn)
+   ```
+   where, from the signer's point of view, `nonceOfPeer` is the nonce it received from the other
+   side and `nonceOfOwn` is the nonce it generated itself. A verifier recomputes the same value by
+   substituting the signer's pubkey and the two nonces from the signer's perspective; the two
+   computations are always identical by construction, so `deriveProof` in `protocol.ts` is one
+   function used both to produce and to verify.
+4. Each side verifies the peer's proof. A mismatch means the peer doesn't hold the same
+   `contentKey` (wrong/foreign list) -- the connection is closed immediately
+   (`E: Proof verification failed`).
+5. **Membership check**: the peer's pubkey must be an active (non-removed) member of the locally
+   held member directory for this list.
+   - **Open list, peer unknown**: this is the "join without internet" path from
+     `docs/KONZEPT.md` section 3 -- a device that just scanned an invite QR code, with no
+     connectivity yet, still proves it holds the list's content key (step 3 above), which for an
+     **open** list is exactly the same proof an invite grants. The peer is accepted and added to
+     the local member directory on the spot.
+   - **Managed list, peer unknown**: the connection is closed. Joining a managed list without
+     internet access is out of scope for this round (an admin device is needed to accept the join
+     request over a relay) -- logged, not silently ignored.
+6. Both sides are now verified; each sends its **Summary**: every item's `(dTag, maxFieldTime)`
+   (including tombstoned/deleted items, so deletions still propagate) plus the locally held
+   directory event's `created_at` (0 if none). `maxFieldTime` is
+   `max(openT, detailT, categoryT, iconT, deletedT)` in local device-clamped milliseconds (the
+   same clamped timestamps used for last-writer-wins merging, see `lists/core/merge.ts`).
+7. On receiving the peer's Summary (which may arrive before this side has sent its own -- both
+   directions are independent), each side computes its outgoing diff: for every local item whose
+   `maxFieldTime` is strictly newer than what the peer's Summary reports (or that's altogether
+   missing from the peer's Summary), it builds a **fresh signed event from its own current merged
+   state** (see "Events" below) and sends it as an **Event** message. If the local directory's
+   timestamp is newer than the peer's, the locally held signed **Directory** event is forwarded
+   too.
+8. Once a side has sent every event from its diff, it sends **Done**.
+9. Once both sides have sent and received Done, the session is **active**: from here on it behaves
+   like a live connection (see "Live sync" below).
+
+### Handshake timeout
+
+If the handshake (steps 1-5) hasn't completed within **15 seconds** of the connection being
+established, the session is closed.
+
+## Events: why no event store is needed
+
+An item's Event, whether sent during the initial diff or later as a live push, is **not** a
+forwarded copy of some other device's original signed event. Instead, each device builds its own
+event from its own currently merged state, signed with its own device key:
+
+```
+event = buildItemEvent(ownDeviceSecretKey, listPubkey, dTag, contentKey, currentMergedPayload)
+```
+
+This is valid because the merge (`lists/core/merge.ts`) is field-wise last-writer-wins keyed by
+each field's own timestamp and originating device, not by which event carried it -- any member is
+allowed to sign an item event for the list (see `lists/core/events.ts#buildItemEvent`), and the
+receiving side runs it through the exact same signature + membership + merge pipeline it uses for
+relay-sourced events (`lists/sync/listSync.ts`'s `handleItemEvent`, reused via
+`ListsSync#applyIncomingDirectoryEvent` for directories and directly in `nearbyManager.ts` for
+items). So device B can relay device A's changes on to device C without ever having stored A's
+original event -- B just re-signs its own already-merged copy. This also means B->C forwarding
+naturally happens as part of B's own outgoing diff the next time B and C sync, with no separate
+"relay" logic.
+
+The one exception is the **member directory** event, which *is* forwarded as the original signed
+event (not rebuilt), because only the list's own key (open lists) or the admin chain (managed
+lists) may sign it -- see `lists/sync/listSync.ts`'s `directoryRawMetaKey`/`latestDirectoryEvent`,
+which stores the last accepted directory in its original signed form specifically so it can be
+forwarded byte-for-byte over Bluetooth.
+
+## Live sync (after the handshake)
+
+Once a session is `active`:
+
+- **Heartbeat**: every 10 seconds, each side sends a Heartbeat frame (empty payload). Receiving
+  *any* frame (not just Heartbeat) resets the peer's "last seen" timer.
+- **Peer-lost timeout**: if no frame at all has arrived for 30 seconds, the peer is considered
+  lost: the session is closed and the underlying connection is torn down. Scanning (which keeps
+  running the whole time nearby sync is on) will rediscover the device if it's still in range and
+  advertising.
+- **Local push**: when the local store changes (`ListsStore#onChange`), the nearby manager sends
+  the changed item(s) as fresh Event messages (same "rebuild from own merged state" rule as above)
+  to every currently active session for that list, and forwards a new Directory event if the
+  locally held one changed.
+
+## Token rotation, discovery loop, auto-off
+
+- The advertising token rotates every 15 minutes (`TOKEN_ROTATION_MS`); `updateToken()` restarts
+  advertising with the new token (same service UUID, same GATT server, connections are
+  unaffected).
+- Scanning stays on continuously while nearby sync is on for a list (`ScanSettings.SCAN_MODE_LOW_LATENCY`).
+- Nearby sync turns itself off:
+  - after `lists.nearbyAutoOffMinutes` (a setting) minutes with no activity resetting the timer
+    (a peer being found resets it), if that setting is a positive number;
+  - unconditionally after the app has been backgrounded for more than 5 minutes
+    (`BACKGROUND_AUTO_OFF_MS`), regardless of the setting above.
+- Leaving the list's screen does **not** turn nearby sync off by itself (see `docs/KONZEPT.md`
+  section 3: "at the supermarket you might briefly switch apps") -- only the two mechanisms above,
+  or the person tapping the status circle, stop it.
+
+## Errors and permissions
+
+The native module never throws a raw `SecurityException`; a missing permission surfaces as a
+rejected promise with code `E_PERMISSION`. Requesting the right permissions (which differ between
+Android <31 and 31+) is the TS/UI layer's job (`PermissionsAndroid`, see
+`NearbyStatusCircle.tsx`), not the native module's.
+
+## For a future iOS implementation
+
+CoreBluetooth's model maps reasonably closely: `CBPeripheralManager` for advertising + the GATT
+server side, `CBCentralManager`/`CBPeripheral` for scanning + the GATT client side. Two points
+that need extra care compared to Android:
+
+- iOS advertises **only** the service UUID in the background (no arbitrary service-data bytes) --
+  the 8-byte advertising token would need to travel some other way in the background case (e.g. a
+  short exchange right after connecting, before the full handshake), or the feature can be
+  foreground-only on iOS for a first version.
+- CoreBluetooth doesn't expose a MAC address; peers are identified by a `CBPeripheral` identifier
+  that is **not stable** across app launches on iOS the way a BLE MAC is used here as the `address`
+  key throughout this protocol and in `nativeBle.ts`'s `BleDriver` interface. Any iOS
+  `nativeBle.ts`-equivalent needs its own stable-enough key (e.g. the identifier for the lifetime
+  of one nearby-sync session is sufficient, since `address` here is never persisted -- it's purely
+  a transport-session handle).
+
+Everything above the native module boundary -- `protocol.ts`, `session.ts`, `nearbyManager.ts` --
+is platform-independent and would be reused as-is; only a new `nativeBle.ts`-equivalent backed by
+CoreBluetooth (implementing the same `BleDriver` interface) would be needed.
diff --git a/packages/app-mobile/lists/nearby/nativeBle.ts b/packages/app-mobile/lists/nearby/nativeBle.ts
